        <!-- Section 4: Resolution, Bitrate, Framerate & Duration -->
        <section class="editorial-block">
          <h2>How Resolution, Bitrate, Frame Rate, and Codec Dictate File Size</h2>
          <p>
            Digital video file size is governed by an exact mathematical relationship rather than arbitrary software compression. The formula determining raw file size is:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-display-container">
            <div class="formula-equation">
              <code>Video File Size (MB) = [Total Bitrate in kbps × Duration in seconds] ÷ (8 × 1,024)</code>
            </div>
            <p class="formula-caption">
              Total Bitrate equals Video Stream Bitrate plus Audio Stream Bitrate.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Understanding these four core variables helps you dial in the ideal settings for any video project:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Bitrate (Crucial Variable):</strong> Bitrate represents the volume of digital information processed per second of video. Lowering bitrate reduces file size linearly. A 60-second clip encoded at 10,000 kbps (10 Mbps) weighs roughly 75 MB, whereas that identical 60-second clip encoded at 2,000 kbps (2 Mbps) weighs only 15 MB—a dramatic 80% reduction.</li>
            <li><strong>Resolution (Pixel Grid):</strong> A 4K video frame contains 8,294,400 pixels, whereas a 1080p frame contains 2,073,600 pixels (75% fewer pixels). When you downscale from 4K to 1080p, your video encoder requires far fewer bits to represent the scene cleanly.</li>
            <li><strong>Frame Rate (FPS):</strong> A 60 fps video contains twice as many discrete image frames as a 30 fps video. While 60 fps delivers silky-smooth motion for high-speed sports and dancing, it requires roughly 20% to 35% higher bitrate to prevent compression macro-blocking.</li>
            <li><strong>Encoder Codec Efficiency:</strong> Modern codecs like H.264, VP9, and AV1 utilize sophisticated inter-frame prediction (P-frames and B-frames) to store only what changes between frames rather than storing full pictures repeatedly.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

