        <!-- Section 6: Why Compressed Videos Can Sometimes Be Larger -->
        <section class="editorial-block">
          <h2>Why a Compressed Video May Sometimes Turn Out Larger than the Original</h2>
          <p>
            It sounds counterintuitive: you run a video through a compressor, yet the resulting file size increases. This phenomenon is well-understood in video engineering and occurs when a user re-encodes a video that was <strong>already aggressively compressed</strong> at a lower bitrate than the compressor's target preset.
          </p>
          <p>
            For example, if you download a screen recording or an existing WhatsApp video clip that was already compressed to a 600 Kbps bitrate (weighing 4 MB), and you run it through our tool using the <strong>High Quality</strong> preset (which encodes at 3,000 Kbps), the encoder writes 3,000 kilobits per second of data into the output stream. The output file may expand to 15 MB without adding any genuine visual clarity.
          </p>
          <p>
            To prevent this, check your source file size before compressing. If your video is already small (e.g. less than 10 MB for a 30-second clip), select the <strong>Smaller File</strong> preset or keep the original file unchanged.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 7: Browser-Based Privacy Benefits -->
